                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is the first game in the Bejeweled series, and it layed the groundworks and influences of the match-3 genre. Match 3 or more gems in a row or column, make combos (known in the Bejeweled games as Cascades), reach new levels. Unfortunately but understandably, as this the very first game, you're not going to see any gameplay elements like Power Gems or Hypercubes, which you get for matching 4 and 5 respectively. At the same time, there's the standard \"play until you can't make anymore moves\" normal mode, and the time trial mode, where you make as many matches as you can and level up as far as you can within the time limit.\r\n\r\nThe reason why I'm not recommending this is not because of its lack of gameplay elements and game modes, but rather the decision to buy it. I'm not kidding when I say this, but there have been [b]dozens[/b] of reskins, both official and unofficial, and they offer the exact same features and modes as the Deluxe version, which have been properly archived and are freeware, as they were playable online on MSN back in the day. Even the standard, default version was available to play on MSN. Furthermore, the sequel Bejeweled 2 includes the original Bejeweled game mode as an easter egg, complete with the lack of Power Gems and Hypercubes, so you're getting more bang for your buck that way.\r\n\r\nWhile I'm not saying I outright wouldn't recommend this game at all, I will at least say that you can buy it if you know what you're getting into. Again, the game is freeware as it has been playable online for many years with dozens of reskins, so you're not missing out on much. I do recommend buying it if you want to support PopCap, though.",
